s","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Kalmyk translations","Terms with Kango translations","Terms with Kazakh translations","Terms with Komi-Permyak translations","Terms with Komi-Zyrian translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Latin translations","Terms with Limburgish translations","Terms with Malayalam translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Northern Mansi translations","Terms with Persian translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Romanian translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Scots translations","Terms with Sinhalese translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Tagalog translations","Terms with Tamil translations","Terms with Thai translations","Terms with Tundra Nenets translations","Terms with Turkish translations","Terms with Uyghur translations","Terms with Uzbek translations","Terms with Vietnamese translations","en:Mountains","en:Places in Russia"],"id":"4605dc85-2dd7-4ad8-ba03-f193930a5ea0"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"word":"Urals"}]},"uranian":{"id":"4959b635-ca92-4b5e-aa2b-509681bd1f59","word":"Uranian","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or pertaining to the planet Uranus","word":"urânio"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The Uranian year is equal to 84 terrestrial years, or 30687 terrestrial days; and the Uranian day, according to the probable estimate, being shorter than the terrestrial day in the ratio of 1 to 2⁵²⁶⁄₁₀₀₀, it follows that the Uranian year consists of 77336 Uranian days.","ref":"1854, “The Planets: Are They Inhabited Worlds?”, in Dionysius Lardner, editor, The Museum of Science & Art, volume I, number 6, London: Walton and Maberly, […], →OCLC, chapter III, paragraph 8, page 36:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,11],[86,93],[226,233],[257,264]],"id":"d15cc231-6c47-4dfa-9f24-5a18f31d8b42"},{"text":"The inclination of the plane of Uranus's equator to the path in which he travels being about 76°, it follows that the Uranian sun has a range of about 76° on either side of the celestial equator, during the long Uranian year.","ref":"1872, Richard A[nthony] Proctor, “Uranus and Neptune, the Arctic Planets”, in Other Worlds than Ours: The Plurality of Worlds Studied under the Light of Recent Scientific Researches, 3rd edition, London: Longmans, Green, and Co., →OCLC, page 170:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[118,125],[212,219]],"id":"baaad1c3-e8f9-4059-8a9e-8e5921d6ba4e"},{"text":"He had not quite decided whether to be a physicist or a chemist; probably he would have chosen the trans-Uranian borderland between the two.","ref":"1949, John Gunther, “Foreword”, in Death Be Not Proud: A Memoir, New York, N.Y.: Harper & Brothers, →OCLC; Harper Perennial Modern Classics edition, New York, N.Y.: Harper Perennial, 2007, →ISBN, page 9:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[105,112]],"id":"451c6b67-7327-4344-bba1-cea3a9942a70"},{"text":"A decisive argument supporting this hypothesis was the discovery of a remarkable characteristic of the arrangement of the Uranian rings: near the outer edge of the rings there exist several orbits each of which is in resonance with a pair of rings simultaneously.","ref":"1989, A. M. Fridman, N[ick] N. Gor′kavyi, “The Dynamics of Planetary Rings and the Prediction of New Uranian Satellites”, in I[saac] M[arkovich] Khalatnikov, editor, Physics Reviews (Soviet Scientific Reviews, Section A), volume 12, part 4, London: Harwood Academic Publishers, →ISBN, page 326:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[122,129]],"id":"b334defa-80fc-42e3-837e-cb29b45c1e2f"},{"text":"Twice during a uranian year, the rings appear edge-on for a brief period, referred to as a ring plane crossing (RPX).","ref":"2007 September 28, Inke de Pater, H. B. Hammel, Mark R. Showalter, Marcos A. van Dam, “The Dark Side of the Rings of Uranus”, in Science, volume 317, number 5846, Washington, D.C.: American Association for the Advancement of Science, →DOI, →ISSN, →OCLC, page 1888:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[15,22]],"id":"abd198e9-b296-47fd-a6c6-589f2f9fa0e7"}],"topics":["astronomy","natural-sciences"],"attestations":[{"date":"from 1830s","references":[]}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Astronomy"],"links":[["astronomy","astronomy"],["planet","planet"],["Uranus","Uranus"]],"raw_glosses":["(astronomy) Of or pertaining to the planet Uranus."],"glosses":["Of or pertaining to the planet Uranus."],"tags":["not-comparable"],"id":"54ca0872-963b-4737-ad90-d7bea73a1f7a"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"uraeus":{"id":"a8cab175-1661-4ab8-ae7b-539d9b36f8a9","word":"uraeus","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"representation of the sacred asp","tags":["masculine"],"word":"ureu"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"In front is an inlaid cobra, the Royal uræus, and around the band are attached 15 rosettes, each composed of four flowers and four leaves of openwork inlaid.","ref":"20 May 1914, The Times, col. A, page 7:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[39,44]],"id":"c0340580-f768-4d23-8ec5-3f7818f4bad1"},{"text":"even a fair amount of the gold His caravans returned from the Granite Mountains near the Red Sea, were now being fashioned by royal artisans into amulets, breast pieces, gold collars, bracelets, scarabs, uraei, even gold and silver shabti […].","ref":"1983, Norman Mailer, Ancient Evenings:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[204,209]],"id":"cb69e120-c346-4a8a-8041-7fa0cbe4fa83"},{"text":"In the small chamber opposite are Isis and Selket, and Nefertari's cartouche between two uraeuses.","ref":"2004, Michael Haag, Egypt, page 305:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[89,97]],"id":"2240bca6-e3e5-41fd-9e87-d2a03f2223a7"}],"links":[["sacred","sacred"],["asp","asp"],["Egypt","Egypt"]],"categories":["